contentText:
All aboard! A year after his passing, the life and crazy times of late heavy metal legend Ozzy Osbourne will be the subject of the new Ozzy Osbourne: Prince of Darkness haunted house at Universal Studios theme parks during this year&#8217;s annual Halloween Horror Nights.

&#8220;Ozzy Osbourne’s shocking image was as haunting as his heavy metal hits. Now you’ll enter the Prince of Darkness’s kingdom where music and mayhem collide — and everything’s gone off the rails,&#8221; reads a release announcing the attraction. &#8220;Veer through a decaying world ripped straight from Ozzy’s lyrics in this haunted house inspired by one of rock ‘n’ roll’s greatest legends. Let the madness begin!&#8221;

The new attraction will join the raft of other haunted houses at this year&#8217;s Halloween Horror Nights, including ones dedicated to Evil Dead Burn , Hellraiser , season five of Stranger Things and Sinners , as well as Jack & Oddfellow: Chaos & Control, Cybergoria, Invasion Alien: Abduction, MADLANDS: Caged Cannibals and H.R. Bloodengutz Presents: A Halloween Fright-Tacular.

Speaking to EW , Osbourne&#8217;s widow and longtime manager, Sharon Osbourne, said the experience that&#8217;s part of HHN&#8217;s 35th anniversary edition will tell the rocker&#8217;s story from his working class childhood in Birmingham, England to his run as the frontman of Black Sabbath. &#8220;His music throughout every room is at the heart of it all,&#8221; Sharon told EW . &#8220;Fans will also see scenes inspired by Ozzy’s journey, from his early days as a little boy growing up in Birmingham, England, riding the train to and from London, to the iconic imagery and characters from across his career.”

The attraction is described as a &#8220;living horror movie&#8221; that will cover 45 years of Osbourne&#8217;s life, &#8220;transporting guests on a twisted journey though the artist’s outrageous and eccentric solo career.&#8221; This year&#8217;s event will take place from Aug. 28 through Nov. 1, with the Orlando and Hollywood parks reportedly taking different approaches to telling Ozzy&#8217;s story.

Osbourne died on July 22, 2025 at age 76, just weeks after Black Sabbath&#8217;s final concert on July 5. The theme park horror attraction will feature music from throughout Osbourne&#8217;s solo career, including: The Blizzard of Ozz  (1980),  Diary of a Madman  (1981),  Bark at the Moon  (1983),  No More Tears  (1991),  Scream  (2010) and  Ordinary Man  (2020). The L.A. version will take guests on a time trip from Osbourne&#8217;s &#8220;early childhood to the Osbourne Asylum,&#8221; while the Florida one will be a journey through &#8220;ancient ruins and foreboding dungeons before boarding the ‘Crazy Train’ for a twisted ride into a realm inspired by the covers of his fan-favorite albums.&#8221;

Sharon Osbourne told EW that her late husband (whose nicknames included &#8220;Prince of Darkness&#8221;) would have &#8220;absolutely loved it, and I&#8217;m sure he&#8217;s looking down smiling,&#8221; though she noted that there will be no items from Ozzy&#8217;s personal archive, which are &#8220;safely preserved under lock and key.&#8221; She said the concept made her &#8220;incredibly happy&#8221; and the family very proud, with her grandchildren &#8220;counting down the days&#8221; until they can visit.

The whole thing makes perfect sense to Sharon, who said Ozzy would &#8220;absolutely love it. He&#8217;d be laughing his head off,&#8221; considering how much he loved horror movies and Halloween. &#8220;He used to joke that every day was Halloween in our house, so this couldn&#8217;t be more fitting.&#8221;

